Z||000000||Tigger||01-05-2001||10:27 AM||||Check out the Pontiac REV! <A HREF="http://www.gm.com/company/gm_exp_live/calendar_events/00concept/pontiac.htm" TARGET=_blank>Click here</A><P>Even though there are a few things that look, um, well hoaky, it does look cool.  At least it looks a little better than the Aztek anyway.<P>It's going to debut at the Detroit Auto Show coming up shortly.  If I make it down to the show, I'll take some pictures of the real concept car and post them.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue3.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||01-05-2001||11:10 AM||||Gel seats? Sounds interesting. It looks simular to the "dune buggy" concept car they had in the late 80's early 90's. Not sure about the "off-road" lights mounted to the outside of the mirrors. Looks like I'll have to see if they bring it to the Chicago Auto Show.||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||01-05-2001||11:35 AM||||I remeber that 80's buggy. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> That was displayed in green too.(the picture I saw) The one with scuba suit skinned seats, removable instuments so you could hose the sand out of it.....I suppose with another color, I could get used to this REV.  It sounds very functional with some fun thrown in.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||01-05-2001||11:50 AM||||<BR>It does not appear to be my style.  And don't  know if I'll like it, if it ever makes production.  If it winds up looking anything close to the Aztek, we are in real trouble.<P>I don't understand why Pontiac can't develop a low budget two-seat sports car and blow this other crap.  I doubt I would ever buy this REV and I know it would take an act of Congress forcing me to purchase the Aztek.<P>I want to see a low optioned two-seat sports car or roadster.  No power steering, no cup holders. No luxuary items, just smooth lines with a engine/tranny combo that rocks.  I want all muscle, all go, blow this luxuary, off-road C R A P.  I want something that would really give me a kick in the pants at a delicious valve.  Oh wait, I dreamin'. Laters.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 01-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||kslish||01-06-2001||06:04 AM||||This months Automobile says that Pontiac has only been able to sell 4700 Azteks so far since they were introduced. Apparently GM has gotten rid of the team that designed and approved the Aztek for production (thank God).<P>Hopefully there will be a design renaissance at GM shortly.  They have hired Tom Kowaleski and Steve Harris away from DaimlerChrysler and these were the guys that helped Chrysler in the 1990's revamp their lineup with the Viper, Prowler, Intrepid and so on.  GM's 2001 Detroit show cars are the first ones to have been influenced by them.  Of course, I do find it interesting that in addition to the Pontiac REV they are going to show a Oldsmobile convertable show car also with styling by Italy's Bertone coachworks.  Guess someone in upper management failed to tell the design guys that they were dumping Oldsmobile.<P>Since these guys (Kowaleski and Harris) came from Chrysler, I'm hoping one of them had a liking for the Plymouth Pronto Spyder concept car from a few years back.  It was a mid-engined speedster (sort of like the Opel Speedster) with a Neon powertrain and a body made from recycled plastics which had the body color MOLDED-IN to the plastic itself instead of being painted.  Kind of neat.  Imagine what they might be able to create with that ingenuity and the Opel Speedster as a basis to start with.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC||||reg||1
